      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 Association of Veterinary Anaesthetists and the American College of Veterinary Anesthesia and Analgesia.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To determine the thermal and mechanical antinociceptive effects of two different subanesthetic constant rate infusions of racemic ketamine in cats."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Prospective, randomized, blinded, experimental study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "ANIMALS",
            "content": "Eight healthy adult domestic shorthair cats (two intact females and six neutered males)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The thorax and the lower thoracic limbs of each cat were shaved for thermal (TT) and mechanical threshold (MT) testing and a cephalic catheter was placed. Three intravenous treatments of equivalent volume were given as loading dose (LD) followed by an infusion for 2 hours: (K5) 0.5 mg kg(-1) ketamine followed by 5 μg kg(-1) minute(-1) ketamine infusion, (K23) 0.5 mg kg(-1) ketamine followed by 23 μg kg(-1) minute(-1) ketamine infusion or (S) 0.9% saline solution. Effects on behavior, sedation scores, MT and TT were obtained prior to drug treatment and 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.25, 2.5 2.75, 3 hours then every 0.5 hours for 7 hours and 10, 12, 14 and 26 hours after loading dose administration."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Ketamine induced mild sedation for the period of the infusion, no adverse behavioral effects were observed. Thermal threshold was significantly higher than baseline (K5: 44.5 ± 0.7 °C; K23: 44.5 ± 0.5 °C) at 15 minutes in the K5 group (46.8 ± 3.5 °C) and at 45 minutes in the K23 group (47.1 ± 4.1 °C). In the K23 group TT was significantly increased compared to S and K5 at 45 minutes. In K5 at 15 minutes MT (9.6 ± 4.0 N) was different to baseline (6.1 ± 0.8 N) and to the S group (5.9 ± 2.3 N)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION AND CLINICAL RELEVANCE",
            "content": "Low dose rate ketamine infusions minimally affect thermal and mechanical antinociception in cats. Further studies with different nociceptive testing methods are necessary to assess whether ketamine could be a useful analgesic in cats."
          }
        ]
      },
